WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.369 --> 00:00:04.536
- I broke my neck, I beat
cancer and I'm going to Rio.

00:00:05.924 --> 00:00:07.769
This is the first time that women's rugby

00:00:07.769 --> 00:00:09.380
will be in the Olympics.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.667
I'm Jillion Potter and I
play rugby for Team USA.

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:12.500
USA

00:00:16.652 --> 00:00:20.669
Rugby, in particular, is a very
good life lessons teacher,

00:00:20.669 --> 00:00:22.933
so when you get tackled
you have to get back up

00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:27.162
no matter how hard it
is, I'm gonna get up.

00:00:27.162 --> 00:00:30.893
I broke my neck playing rugby in 2010.

00:00:30.893 --> 00:00:34.133
But when I was hit I felt and I heard

00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:36.221
just pop, pop, pop, pop, pop,

00:00:36.221 --> 00:00:38.867
and I knew immediately
something was wrong.

00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:40.332
When the doctors came in and told me

00:00:40.332 --> 00:00:42.942
I might not ever be able
to play rugby again,

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:45.595
I like tried to silently cry for awhile.

00:00:45.595 --> 00:00:48.323
I went through this like
period of allowing myself

00:00:48.323 --> 00:00:51.827
to, to be upset and then being
like get it together Jillion,

00:00:51.827 --> 00:00:54.345
like you can be strong in this moment.

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:56.753
There was about a year
between when I broke my neck

00:00:56.753 --> 00:00:58.244
and when I was able to
get back on the field

00:00:58.244 --> 00:01:01.773
with my team and then in June of 2014,

00:01:01.773 --> 00:01:05.098
I was diagnosed with a very
rare soft tissue cancer.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:08.071
I had chemo and radiation for seven months

00:01:08.071 --> 00:01:10.321
and I lost about 20 pounds.

00:01:11.544 --> 00:01:12.823
Throughout the treatment I was actually

00:01:12.823 --> 00:01:15.415
able to workout and that was something

00:01:15.415 --> 00:01:17.327
that I was pretty adamant about.

00:01:17.327 --> 00:01:19.686
Thankfully the chemotherapy worked

00:01:19.686 --> 00:01:21.624
and I'm cancer-free,
but it could have easily

00:01:21.624 --> 00:01:23.735
turned the other way and I don't think

00:01:23.735 --> 00:01:26.709
I could even imagine
what that would be like,

00:01:26.709 --> 00:01:28.806
but I would hope that I would still

00:01:28.806 --> 00:01:31.088
have that attitude of never giving up

00:01:31.088 --> 00:01:35.154
and rugby was instrumental
in getting me back

00:01:35.154 --> 00:01:36.646
to where I am today.

00:01:36.646 --> 00:01:39.660
The rugby community, they really helped me

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.589
stay positive and keep working out,

00:01:41.589 --> 00:01:44.545
keep that fire to say I'm gonna be back

00:01:44.545 --> 00:01:47.151
and I'm gonna be ready for Rio.

00:01:47.151 --> 00:01:49.734
(upbeat music)

